1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,210
In 2017, a humanoid robot did a backflip.

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:08,727
In 2024, a company called Unitree posted

3
00:00:08,727 --> 00:00:12,629
a video of its electric humanoid doing one too.

4
00:00:13,049 --> 00:00:15,915
Now ask one of these robots to fold a shirt.

5
00:00:16,185 --> 00:00:20,677
In February 2026, a startup began shipping a laundry-folding

6
00:00:20,677 --> 00:00:25,392
robot for nearly eight thousand dollars. When it gets stuck,

7
00:00:25,392 --> 00:00:29,847
a human specialist takes over remotely, for a few seconds at a time.

8
00:00:30,267 --> 00:00:33,049
A backflip makes headlines. Folding

9
00:00:33,049 --> 00:00:35,532
laundry is a chore a child can do.

10
00:00:35,852 --> 00:00:39,646
So why has the stunt been solved, and the chore has not?

11
00:00:40,166 --> 00:00:42,888
The answer was written down in 1988,

12
00:00:42,888 --> 00:00:46,158
and it says the backflip is the easy part.

13
00:00:46,978 --> 00:00:52,351
In 1988, the roboticist Hans Moravec noticed something strange.

14
00:00:52,621 --> 00:00:56,473
In his book Mind Children, he argued that it is far easier

15
00:00:56,473 --> 00:00:59,803
to make a computer play checkers at an adult level

16
00:00:59,803 --> 00:01:04,031
than to give it the perception and mobility of a one-year-old.

17
00:01:04,301 --> 00:01:08,371
Nine years later, in 1997, IBM's Deep Blue beat

18
00:01:08,371 --> 00:01:13,045
the world chess champion, Garry Kasparov, in a match.

19
00:01:13,465 --> 00:01:18,008
This became known as Moravec's paradox: what is hard for people

20
00:01:18,008 --> 00:01:23,157
is often easy for machines, and what is easy for people is often hard.

21
00:01:23,427 --> 00:01:27,141
Why? Moravec's explanation was evolution.

22
00:01:27,411 --> 00:01:31,153
Seeing and moving have been refined for hundreds of millions

23
00:01:31,153 --> 00:01:35,929
of years. Fossil eyes date back more than 500 million years.

24
00:01:36,249 --> 00:01:38,944
Writing is about five thousand years old.

25
00:01:39,214 --> 00:01:43,704
Reasoning feels hard because it is new. Seeing and moving feel

26
00:01:43,704 --> 00:01:47,700
easy only because evolution has polished them for so long.

27
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:53,139
A robot gets no such head start. So the real question is not why

28
00:01:53,139 --> 00:01:57,528
robots are bad at laundry. It is why they are good at backflips.

29
00:01:58,348 --> 00:02:01,235
Here is the first surprise. A backflip

30
00:02:01,235 --> 00:02:03,846
is easy in the same way chess is easy.

31
00:02:04,116 --> 00:02:07,792
A humanoid robot is a stack of rigid metal links

32
00:02:07,792 --> 00:02:11,563
joined at motors. Physics engines handle rigid

33
00:02:11,563 --> 00:02:14,743
bodies well, so a computer can simulate one.

34
00:02:15,013 --> 00:02:19,001
That means engineers can train the robot in a simulator, where

35
00:02:19,001 --> 00:02:23,232
it can fall as many times as it takes without breaking anything.

36
00:02:23,502 --> 00:02:28,861
In 2021, researchers at ETH Zurich and NVIDIA taught a four-legged

37
00:02:28,861 --> 00:02:34,059
robot to walk over rough ground in about 20 minutes, on a single

38
00:02:34,059 --> 00:02:38,958
graphics card, using thousands of simulated robots at once.

39
00:02:39,228 --> 00:02:43,218
Then the trained controller is copied onto the real machine.

40
00:02:43,218 --> 00:02:48,153
Reports say Unitree's 2024 backflip was trained that way too.

41
00:02:48,423 --> 00:02:51,814
A backflip is also short, rehearsed, and flat.

42
00:02:51,814 --> 00:02:55,050
The floor is known, the body is known, and nothing

43
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:58,086
in the scene moves unless the robot moves it.

44
00:02:58,406 --> 00:03:02,070
It is an exam where the questions are published in advance.

45
00:03:02,690 --> 00:03:07,490
So here is the twist. A backflip is a hard stunt with an easy

46
00:03:07,490 --> 00:03:12,009
problem inside it: a known body, a known floor, a known goal.

47
00:03:12,329 --> 00:03:15,386
Laundry is the reverse. An easy-looking

48
00:03:15,386 --> 00:03:17,723
job with a hard problem inside it.

49
00:03:18,543 --> 00:03:21,514
Start with the cloth. A metal link has one

50
00:03:21,514 --> 00:03:24,497
shape. A shirt has no fixed shape at all.

51
00:03:24,767 --> 00:03:28,126
Robotics researchers say a deformable object

52
00:03:28,126 --> 00:03:31,590
has, in effect, infinite degrees of freedom.

53
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:35,344
And cloth hides itself. A fold covers a sleeve,

54
00:03:35,344 --> 00:03:38,287
and the camera cannot see what is underneath.

55
00:03:38,557 --> 00:03:42,488
Simulating cloth is the other trouble. Studies report

56
00:03:42,488 --> 00:03:45,939
that simulated garments stretch, distort, and pass

57
00:03:45,939 --> 00:03:48,915
through themselves, and that accurate cloth

58
00:03:48,915 --> 00:03:52,816
simulators are often too slow for large-scale training.

59
00:03:53,136 --> 00:03:57,027
So the trick that taught the backflip works much less well on a shirt.

60
00:03:57,297 --> 00:04:00,180
Then there is your home. Every kitchen,

61
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:03,560
every closet, every laundry basket is different.

62
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:06,638
And there are the hands. A human hand

63
00:04:06,638 --> 00:04:10,198
has about 17,000 receptors for light touch.

64
00:04:10,468 --> 00:04:13,728
Rodney Brooks, a co-founder of iRobot, argues

65
00:04:13,728 --> 00:04:18,374
that you cannot learn touch if nobody is collecting touch data.

66
00:04:18,794 --> 00:04:24,617
In 2019, OpenAI trained a robot hand in simulation to solve a Rubik's

67
00:04:24,617 --> 00:04:30,121
Cube, with training equal to roughly 13,000 years of experience.

68
00:04:30,391 --> 00:04:33,927
A separate program worked out the moves. The hand only

69
00:04:33,927 --> 00:04:38,274
had to carry them out. It still solved the cube just 60 percent

70
00:04:38,274 --> 00:04:41,875
of the time, and 20 percent for the hardest scrambles.

71
00:04:42,295 --> 00:04:48,989
In 2010, a robot at UC Berkeley folded towels, 50 times out of 50.

72
00:04:48,989 --> 00:04:53,611
But a towel is a rectangle with four corners. A shirt is not.

73
00:04:53,881 --> 00:04:58,146
In 2026, a laundry robot on sale reportedly cannot

74
00:04:58,146 --> 00:05:01,754
handle bed sheets or inside-out garments.

75
00:05:02,174 --> 00:05:05,096
The robot is not failing at the physics.

76
00:05:05,096 --> 00:05:08,149
It is failing at the variety, and at the practice.

77
00:05:08,969 --> 00:05:13,499
Which brings us to the real bottleneck. Not the motors. The data.

78
00:05:13,769 --> 00:05:17,623
Chatbots learned from text, and there is a lot of text.

79
00:05:17,623 --> 00:05:22,232
Meta's Llama 3 was trained on more than 15 trillion tokens.

80
00:05:22,502 --> 00:05:27,230
A token is roughly three-quarters of a word. At the average

81
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:31,365
adult reading speed, 238 words a minute, reading

82
00:05:31,365 --> 00:05:35,596
that much nonstop would take about 90,000 years.

83
00:05:36,016 --> 00:05:40,066
Now the robots. The Open X-Embodiment project

84
00:05:40,066 --> 00:05:43,435
pooled data from 21 institutions into more

85
00:05:43,435 --> 00:05:48,160
than a million robot trajectories, from 22 kinds of robot.

86
00:05:48,430 --> 00:05:53,270
A million sounds like a lot. But each trajectory is one short attempt

87
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:58,685
at a task. The DROID dataset, collected by 50 people over 12

88
00:05:58,685 --> 00:06:05,099
months, has 76,000 trajectories. That adds up to about 350 hours.

89
00:06:05,369 --> 00:06:11,087
AgiBot World, from 2025, lists almost 3,000 hours of recordings,

90
00:06:11,087 --> 00:06:16,935
gathered by over a hundred robots. That is 124 days.

91
00:06:17,455 --> 00:06:21,593
Ninety thousand years of text, against four months of robot.

92
00:06:21,863 --> 00:06:25,419
A token and an hour of robot time are not the same

93
00:06:25,419 --> 00:06:30,121
thing. But in time, the gap is more than 200,000 to one.

94
00:06:30,541 --> 00:06:34,745
So where does robot data come from? There are three places.

95
00:06:35,015 --> 00:06:39,063
First, teleoperation. A person drives the robot through

96
00:06:39,063 --> 00:06:42,751
a task while it records. Stanford's Mobile ALOHA

97
00:06:42,751 --> 00:06:46,590
team found that 50 demonstrations per task, combined

98
00:06:46,590 --> 00:06:50,139
with older data, could raise success rates by up

99
00:06:50,139 --> 00:06:54,231
to 90 percent, enough for tasks like cooking a shrimp.

100
00:06:54,501 --> 00:06:58,069
It works, but it is slow by design: an hour of data

101
00:06:58,069 --> 00:07:01,062
costs at least an hour of someone's time.

102
00:07:01,332 --> 00:07:03,476
Second, simulation, which we saw

103
00:07:03,476 --> 00:07:06,254
works for backflips and badly for cloth.

104
00:07:06,524 --> 00:07:10,778
Third, video of people. Figure says a model trained only

105
00:07:10,778 --> 00:07:15,319
on first-person human video could navigate cluttered homes.

106
00:07:15,319 --> 00:07:19,205
But video has no touch in it, and that is Brooks's objection.

107
00:07:20,025 --> 00:07:23,268
This is why robot demos are so hard to read.

108
00:07:23,538 --> 00:07:28,565
In October 2024, Tesla showed Optimus robots serving drinks

109
00:07:28,565 --> 00:07:33,036
at its We, Robot event. Bloomberg reported that people

110
00:07:33,036 --> 00:07:37,354
in another location oversaw many of the interactions, and a Tesla

111
00:07:37,354 --> 00:07:41,460
official later said some functions were remotely controlled.

112
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:46,905
In October 2025, a reporter at the Wall Street Journal tested

113
00:07:46,905 --> 00:07:51,843
a home humanoid called Neo, priced at 20,000 dollars.

114
00:07:51,843 --> 00:07:55,793
In her test, the work was done by a remote human operator.

115
00:07:56,063 --> 00:08:00,247
That is not necessarily a trick. Some companies say openly

116
00:08:00,247 --> 00:08:04,822
that the human is part of the plan. 1X's chief executive told

117
00:08:04,822 --> 00:08:09,500
her that without customers' data, the product cannot get better.

118
00:08:09,770 --> 00:08:11,989
The laundry startup says the same:

119
00:08:11,989 --> 00:08:15,593
its specialists' corrections feed back into its model.

120
00:08:16,013 --> 00:08:19,241
The remote human is a stopgap. And it is also

121
00:08:19,241 --> 00:08:21,165
where the missing data comes from.

122
00:08:21,585 --> 00:08:25,133
So when you watch a robot demo, ask three questions.

123
00:08:25,403 --> 00:08:29,424
Is it autonomous, or is someone driving? Is the video

124
00:08:29,424 --> 00:08:32,731
at real speed? And how many tries did it take?

125
00:08:33,051 --> 00:08:37,644
A robot that fails one time in ten still needs someone watching.

126
00:08:38,464 --> 00:08:42,333
So when will a robot fold your laundry at home, on its own?

127
00:08:42,653 --> 00:08:46,011
Here is my bet. It depends on two things.

128
00:08:46,281 --> 00:08:51,565
One: data from real homes. Not thousands of hours. Millions.

129
00:08:51,565 --> 00:08:55,978
Probably collected by robots that are partly driven by people today.

130
00:08:56,248 --> 00:09:01,128
Two: reliability. The day the remote helper is no longer

131
00:09:01,128 --> 00:09:06,378
needed is the day it works 99 times out of 100, not 9 out of 10.

132
00:09:06,648 --> 00:09:10,127
Robots that fold some clothes, with a human on call,

133
00:09:10,127 --> 00:09:13,888
are already for sale. A robot that handles your whole

134
00:09:13,888 --> 00:09:18,012
household? Rodney Brooks has written that dexterity

135
00:09:18,012 --> 00:09:21,722
will still lag far behind human hands past 2036.

136
00:09:22,142 --> 00:09:26,183
My bet sits in between. A supervised robot that folds

137
00:09:26,183 --> 00:09:30,074
most of your laundry by the end of this decade. One

138
00:09:30,074 --> 00:09:34,454
that does it with nobody watching, not before the mid-2030s.

139
00:09:34,724 --> 00:09:37,725
A signal to watch: the day a company stops

140
00:09:37,725 --> 00:09:40,767
saying remote operator in the fine print.

141
00:09:41,187 --> 00:09:44,430
The lesson is bigger than laundry. What looks

142
00:09:44,430 --> 00:09:47,838
easy to us is easy because evolution did the work,

143
00:09:47,838 --> 00:09:50,690
and nobody wrote that work down as data.

144
00:09:51,110 --> 00:09:54,679
So the next time you see a robot do a backflip, don't ask

145
00:09:54,679 --> 00:09:58,425
how it got so good. Ask what it was never asked to do.

146
00:09:59,045 --> 00:10:02,891
Which chore would you hand to a robot first? And would you let

147
00:10:02,891 --> 00:10:06,673
a stranger drive it while you are out? Tell me in the comments.

